 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# Predicate pushdown not working correctly for multi-table queries\n\nI'm using SQLGlot's optimizer to push down predicates in my SQL queries, but I've noticed that it's not working correctly for certain types of queries.\n\n## Description\n\nI have a query that joins multiple tables and includes a WHERE clause with conditions on columns from these tables. When I try to optimize this query using the predicate pushdown feature, the predicates aren't being pushed down correctly.\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```sql\nSELECT a.id, b.name \nFROM table_a a\nJOIN table_b b ON a.id = b.id\nWHERE a.value > 10 AND b.status = 'active'\n```\n\nAfter optimization, I would expect the predicates to be pushed down to their respective tables, but this isn't happening. The optimizer seems to be treating multi-table predicates differently than single-table ones.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nI created a more complex example to reproduce this issue:\n\n```python\nfrom sqlglot import parse_one, optimizer\n\nsql = \"\"\"\nSELECT o.order_id, c.customer_name, p.product_name\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nJOIN products p ON o.product_id = p.product_id\nWHERE o.order_date > '2023-01-01' AND c.region = 'North' AND p.category = 'Electronics'\n\"\"\"\n\n# Parse and optimize the query\nquery = parse_one(sql)\noptimized = optimizer.optimize(query)\n\nprint(optimized.sql(pretty=True))\n```\n\nThe optimized query doesn't push down the predicates to the individual tables as expected. This makes queries less efficient, especially when working with large datasets where predicate pushdown could significantly improve performance.\n</issue>\n",
